> What does the TOMRenderer do?


⏺ Great question! The TOMRenderer is the bridge between the DOM tree and the actual terminal output. It's where the magic happens.

  TOMRenderer Responsibilities

  1. Tree Walking & Element Dispatching

  class TOMRenderer {
    render(rootElement: Element): void {
      // Walk the DOM tree and call each element's render method
      this.walkTree(rootElement, (element) => {
        if (element instanceof TOMElement) {
          element.render(this.screenBuffer);
        }
      });

      // Flush everything to terminal
      this.screenBuffer.render();
    }

    private walkTree(node: Element, callback: (element: Element) => void): void {
      callback(node);
      for (const child of node.children) {
        this.walkTree(child, callback);
      }
    }
  }

  2. Layout Coordination

  class TOMRenderer {
    render(rootElement: Element): void {
      // 1. Layout pass - calculate positions/sizes
      this.layoutEngine.computeLayout(rootElement, this.terminalWidth, this.terminalHeight);

      // 2. Render pass - draw to screen buffer
      this.renderTree(rootElement);

      // 3. Output pass - convert to ANSI and display
      this.screenBuffer.render();
    }
  }

  3. ScreenBuffer Management

  class TOMRenderer {
    private screenBuffer: ScreenBuffer;
    private containerBuffers = new Map<TOMContainer, ScreenBuffer>();

    renderElement(element: TOMElement): void {
      if (element instanceof TOMContainer) {
        // Containers get their own buffer
        const buffer = this.getOrCreateBuffer(element);
        element.renderSelf(buffer);

        // Composite onto parent buffer
        this.compositeBuffer(buffer, element.bounds);
      } else {
        // Text/leaf elements render directly to parent buffer
        const parentBuffer = this.findParentBuffer(element);
        element.renderSelf(parentBuffer);
      }
    }
  }

  4. Event System Bridge

  class TOMRenderer {
    constructor(document: TOMDocument) {
      this.setupInputHandling();
      this.setupMutationObserver(document);
    }

    private setupInputHandling(): void {
      process.stdin.on('data', (buffer) => {
        const input = buffer.toString();

        if (this.isMouseInput(input)) {
          const {x, y, button} = this.parseMouseInput(input);
          const targetElement = this.hitTest(x, y);
          targetElement?.dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent('click', {x, y, button}));
        } else {
          const key = this.parseKeyInput(input);
          const focusedElement = this.getFocusedElement();
          focusedElement?.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent('keydown', {key}));
        }
      });
    }

    private setupMutationObserver(document: TOMDocument): void {
      const observer = new MutationObserver(() => {
        this.scheduleRender(); // Re-render on DOM changes
      });

      observer.observe(document.body, {
        childList: true,
        subtree: true,
        attributes: true,
        characterData: true
      });
    }
  }

  What TOMRenderer Does NOT Do

  The renderer is NOT responsible for:
  - ❌ Creating DOM elements (that's TOMDocument)
  - ❌ Handling element-specific rendering logic (that's each TOMElement)
  - ❌ Layout calculations (that's Yoga)
  - ❌ Style computation (that's TOMStyleEngine)

  Element-Specific Rendering

  Each element handles its own rendering:

  class TOMText extends TOMElement {
    renderSelf(buffer: ScreenBuffer): void {
      const styled = BunTextUtils.styleText(this.textContent, this.style);
      buffer.put(this.bounds.x, this.bounds.y, styled);
    }
  }

  class TOMButton extends TOMContainer {
    renderSelf(buffer: ScreenBuffer): void {
      // Render button background
      buffer.fill(this.bounds, ' ', {
        bgColor: this.style.backgroundColor,
        fgColor: this.style.color
      });

      // Render button border
      this.renderBorder(buffer);

      // Let children render themselves
      super.renderSelf(buffer);
    }
  }

  The Flow

  1. DOM changes (appendChild, style changes, etc.)
  2. MutationObserver fires
  3. TOMRenderer.scheduleRender()
  4. Layout pass (Yoga calculates positions/sizes)
  5. Render pass (each element renders to buffers)
  6. Composite pass (combine all buffers)
  7. Output pass (ScreenBuffer → ANSI → terminal)

  So the TOMRenderer is really the orchestrator - it coordinates layout, manages screen buffers, handles input/output, and
  triggers re-renders when the DOM changes.

  The actual drawing logic lives in each element type! 🎯


