  What is a ScreenBuffer?

  Think of it like a canvas or 2D array that represents what should be on screen:

  // Conceptually, a ScreenBuffer is just:
  class SimpleScreenBuffer {
    private cells: Cell[][];

    constructor(width: number, height: number) {
      // Create a 2D grid of "cells"
      this.cells = Array(height).fill(null).map(() =>
        Array(width).fill(null).map(() => ({
          char: ' ',
          color: 'white',
          bgColor: 'black'
        }))
      );
    }

    // Put a character at position (x, y)
    put(x: number, y: number, char: string, color?: string) {
      this.cells[y][x] = { char, color: color || 'white', bgColor: 'black' };
    }

    // Convert the buffer to ANSI and print it
    render() {
      for (let y = 0; y < this.cells.length; y++) {
        let line = '';
        for (let x = 0; x < this.cells[y].length; x++) {
          const cell = this.cells[y][x];
          line += Bun.color(cell.color, 'ansi') + cell.char;
        }
        console.log(line + '\x1b[0m'); // Reset colors at end of line
      }
    }
  }

  Why is this useful?

  Without ScreenBuffer (direct terminal writing):
  // Messy! Hard to manage!
  process.stdout.write('\x1b[10;5H'); // Move to (5, 10)
  process.stdout.write('\x1b[31mHello\x1b[0m'); // Red text
  process.stdout.write('\x1b[12;5H'); // Move to (5, 12)
  process.stdout.write('\x1b[34mWorld\x1b[0m'); // Blue text

  With ScreenBuffer (clean separation):
  // Clean! Manageable!
  const buffer = new ScreenBuffer(80, 24);
  buffer.put(5, 10, 'Hello', 'red');
  buffer.put(5, 12, 'World', 'blue');
  buffer.render(); // Converts to ANSI and displays

  Benefits of ScreenBuffer:

  1. Separation of concerns - Build your UI, then render it
  2. Compositing - Layer multiple buffers on top of each other
  3. Clipping - Only draw within certain boundaries
  4. Dirty tracking - Only redraw what changed
  5. Testing - Easy to test without actually printing to terminal

  You don't need to be an ANSI expert!

  The beauty is that ScreenBuffer hides the ANSI complexity. You just:
  1. Put characters/colors at positions
  2. Call render()
  3. ScreenBuffer handles all the cursor movement and ANSI codes

  Think of it like drawing on a canvas in a game - you don't need to understand GPU commands, you just draw pixels! 🎨

