⏺ Perfect! I've successfully implemented mouse support for TOM. Here's what was added:

  Mouse Support Features:

  1. TOMMouseHandler class that:
    - Enables/disables terminal mouse tracking
    - Parses SGR extended mouse sequences
    - Converts terminal coordinates to DOM MouseEvents
    - Tracks hover state and generates mouseenter/mouseleave events
    - Handles clicks, mouse movement, and wheel events
    - Finds elements at specific coordinates
  2. Integration with TOMDocument:
    - enableMouse() and disableMouse() methods
    - Mouse input is processed alongside keyboard input
    - Uses HappyDOM's MouseEvent classes for compatibility
  3. Mouse events supported:
    - mousedown - button press
    - mouseup - button release
    - click - generated after mouseup
    - mousemove - cursor movement
    - mouseenter - entering an element
    - mouseleave - leaving an element
    - wheel - scroll wheel events
  4. Demo created showing:
    - Hover effects on buttons
    - Click handling with visual feedback
    - Real-time mouse position tracking
    - Integration with keyboard input (Q to quit)

  The mouse support uses standard DOM event APIs, so existing web development patterns work
  perfectly:

  button.addEventListener('click', () => {
    console.log('Button clicked!');
  });

  button.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {
    button.style.backgroundColor = 'yellow';
  });

  This brings TOM much closer to a complete DOM implementation for terminals, supporting both
  keyboard and mouse interactions just like a web browser!


