⏺ Ah, that's a brilliant insight! You're absolutely right. Since TOM doesn't have true "block
  layout" (no concept of document flow, line boxes, etc.), we can treat inline-flex semantics as
  just another layout mode.

  The Key Insight

  In web browsers:
  - display: block = takes full width, stacks vertically
  - display: inline = flows horizontally, wraps at container edge
  - display: inline-flex = flex container that flows inline

  In TOM terminals:
  - No inherent "block" behavior - everything is positioned explicitly
  - Flexbox is our primary layout - we're already doing flex-like positioning
  - Inline-flex could just mean "flex container that participates in text flow"

  Semantic Approach

  Instead of building separate layout engines, we could:

  // Container that uses flex layout internally
  <container style={{display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'row'}}>
    <text>Hello </text>

    // This inline-flex container flows with the text
    <container style={{display: 'inline-flex', gap: 5}}>
      <button>Bold</button>
      <button>Italic</button>
    </container>

    <text> more text</text>
  </container>

  Implementation Strategy

  1. Extend current flex system - add display: 'inline-flex'
  2. Inline-flex semantics - these containers participate in horizontal text flow
  3. Text wrapping - when line exceeds width, wrap inline-flex containers too
  4. Unified layout - same Yoga-based engine handles both flex and inline-flex

  Benefits

  - Consistent API - same flex properties work for both display types
  - No separate engines - one layout system to maintain
  - Natural behavior - inline-flex containers can contain buttons, icons, etc. within text
  - Simpler architecture - avoid the complexity of separate block/inline engines

