> Right so every inline node has a content width, and maximum width determined by layout, but
  because of word-wrapping, the inline node should always share the maximum width, but the width
  of the inline-node can determine the width of siblings, so how do we handle all this?


⏺ Excellent question! This is the core challenge of inline layout - it's a two-pass coordination
  problem between horizontal text flow and container constraints.

  🧩 The Inline Layout Challenge:

  Container width: 80 chars
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Text before [Button A] [Button B] more text that might wrap to next line...    │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Each inline element needs to know:
  1. Content width - minimum space needed for its content
  2. Available width - space remaining on current line
  3. Siblings' impact - how much space other inline elements consume

  🔄 Two-Pass Solution:

  Pass 1: Measure Phase

  interface InlineNode {
    type: 'text' | 'element';
    contentWidth: number;    // Minimum width needed
    preferredWidth: number;  // Ideal width if unconstrained
    element?: TOMElement;
    text?: string;
  }

  // Measure all inline children first
  const inlineNodes: InlineNode[] = [];
  for (const child of container.childNodes) {
    if (child.nodeType === 3) { // Text node
      inlineNodes.push({
        type: 'text',
        text: child.textContent,
        contentWidth: Bun.stringWidth(child.textContent),
        preferredWidth: Bun.stringWidth(child.textContent)
      });
    } else if (child.display === 'inline-flex') {
      const measured = measureInlineElement(child);
      inlineNodes.push({
        type: 'element',
        element: child,
        contentWidth: measured.minWidth,
        preferredWidth: measured.preferredWidth
      });
    }
  }

  Pass 2: Layout Phase

  let currentLine: InlineNode[] = [];
  let currentX = 0;
  const lines: InlineNode[][] = [];

  for (const node of inlineNodes) {
    // Can this node fit on current line?
    if (currentX + node.contentWidth <= containerWidth) {
      currentLine.push(node);
      currentX += node.contentWidth;
    } else {
      // Start new line
      lines.push(currentLine);
      currentLine = [node];
      currentX = node.contentWidth;
    }
  }

  🎯 Key Insights:

  1. Inline-flex containers get their contentWidth from flexbox layout of their children
  2. Text nodes can break/wrap, but inline-flex containers are atomic units
  3. Line building happens left-to-right, wrapping when content exceeds maxWidth
  4. Each line distributes available width among its inline nodes

  🛠️ Implementation Strategy:

  class InlineLayoutEngine {
    layoutInlineContent(container: TOMElement, maxWidth: number): LineBox[] {
      // Pass 1: Measure all inline children
      const nodes = this.measureInlineNodes(container.childNodes, maxWidth);

      // Pass 2: Build lines with wrapping
      const lines = this.buildLines(nodes, maxWidth);

      // Pass 3: Position elements within each line
      return this.positionLines(lines, container.bounds);
    }
  }

  This gives us proper text wrapping that respects inline container boundaries while allowing
  mixed content to flow naturally!

  The key is that inline-flex containers are measured first, then text flows around their fixed
  dimensions.


