> Can you explain the difference between fullscreen/flow/window?

⏺ Absolutely! Here's the breakdown of the three viewport modes:

  Fullscreen Mode (createTOM())

  - Takes over entire terminal - clears screen, positions at (0,0)
  - Fixed position - content renders from top-left of terminal
  - Document height = viewport height - fills whole screen
  - Use cases: TUI apps like vim, htop, less
  - Example: Your text editor, file manager, dashboard

  const tom = createTOM(); // Full terminal takeover

  Flow Mode (createTOMFlow())

  - Renders inline with terminal output - doesn't clear screen
  - Relative position - appends to current cursor position
  - Height = 'auto' - only uses space needed for content
  - Use cases: Progress bars, test output, CLI tool feedback
  - Example: Jest test results, build progress, status messages

  const tom = createTOMFlow(); // Renders inline
  // Terminal output continues normally after

  Window Mode (createTOMWindow())

  - Fixed-size box in terminal flow - like a mini viewport
  - Relative position - renders at current position but with set height
  - Can scroll if content exceeds window height
  - Use cases: Log viewers, menus, preview panes
  - Example: npm install output box, selection menus

  const tom = createTOMWindow({ height: 10 }); // 10-line box

  Think of it like CSS positioning:
  - Fullscreen = position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100vw; height: 100vh;
  - Flow = position: relative; height: auto;
  - Window = position: relative; height: 200px; overflow: auto;

